          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What TSH range does guidance describe as the treatment threshold in subclinical hypothyroidism?",
          "answer": "Clinical guidelines describe broad agreement on treating subclinical hypothyroidism when the thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) is persistently above about 10 mIU/L.\n\nIn the TSH band between the upper reference limit and 10 mIU/L, guidance differs:\n*   Some guidance recommends a trial of treatment if symptoms, positive antibodies, or cardiovascular risk are present.\n*   Other guidance suggests monitoring alone for most individuals, particularly in older adults where trials have not shown symptomatic benefit from treatment.\n\nAdditionally, guidelines may differ on whether to adjust the reference range upward for older adults, as a mildly raised TSH value might be considered normal for age. Pregnancy is also treated with separate, trimester-specific ranges and a lower treatment threshold.\n\n*From: Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance (educational summary), \"Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change\"*\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
